data: {"type":"TOOL_CALL_RESULT","toolCallId":"call_85283848008d4e688588b190","messageId":"585d2af9-800d-43cc-9ee6-39c5504573d8","content":"{\"ok\":true,\"result\":{\"path\":\"/skills/ag-ui-frontend/SKILL.md\",\"content\":\"1: ---\\n2: name: ag-ui-frontend\\n3: description: Use for frontend consumption of AG-UI messages, shared state, run status, executions, interrupts, and controlled Application Events, Frontend Tools, Agent-controlled frontend capabilities, and browser/client tool execution through project Runtime hooks.\\n4: compatibility: One AG-UI Agent Runtime per generated frontend; Phase 8 permits Plugin source writes while Runtime remains read-only.\\n5: allowed-tools: enable_creator_tools read_file ls glob grep edit_file\\n6: ---\\n7: \\n8: # AG-UI Frontend\\n9: \\n10: Maintain this data flow:\\n11: \\n12: ```text\\n13: AG-UI / Mock transport -> Agent Runtime -> Runtime Context Hooks -> UI Plugin -> project UI stack\\n14: ```\\n15: \\n16: ## Current project contract\\n17: \\n18: - `useAgentMessages()` returns normalized Agent messages, including user, assistant, reasoning, activity, and tool-related messages represented by Runtime Core.\\n19: - `useAgentState<TState>()` returns shared Agent state and must be narrowed safely before property access.\\n20: - `useAgentRun().status` is `idle`, `running`, `awaiting-input`, or `error`; a run error carries the user-visible failure detail.\\n21: - `useAgentExecutions()` exposes each Tool, Reasoning, Step, and Subagent lifecycle directly. Never replace local execution status with a generic Run loading flag.\\n22: - `useAgentInterrupts()` returns only current pending interrupts.\\n23: - `usePluginActions()` exposes instance-scoped Agent commands; `useAgentRuntimeActions()` exposes only Agent Runtime commands. Runtime-only UI state belongs in a named service or runtime store, not in AppUIModel composition.\\n24: - `usePluginEvents()` is the inbound, live-only Application Event channel. Subscribe only to names declared in the Plugin manifest and registered through the application's `Agent.appEventSchemas`.\\n25: - `usePluginService()` remains the component API for named Plugin capabilities.\\n26: - `/agent-contract/agent-tools.ts` owns the explicit set of frontend capability operations advertised to the Agent.\\n27: \\n28: ## Boundaries\\n29: \\n30: - Do not instantiate an AG-UI client or manage a second Agent Runtime inside a Plugin.\\n31: - Do not invent a separate public message or state protocol when the Plugin Contract already exposes AG-UI data.\\n32: - Event, snapshot, delta, streaming, tool-call, and error normalization belongs in the frontend Runtime layer; Plugins render the normalized hook values.\\n33: - Never expose or import AG-UI `CUSTOM`, `CustomEvent`, `BaseEvent`, `RawEvent`, `rawEvent`, or `@ag-ui/core` from Plugin code. Preserve an explicitly supplied backend business event name and payload; never guess an unknown payload shape. Distinguish its consumers: a chat message renderer uses `defineDataMessageUI`, `definition.dataMessageUIs` and `manifest.data.messageUI`, and validates its raw `data`; a live Application Event subscriber uses `Agent.appEventSchemas`, `manifest.data.events` and `usePluginEvents`. Do not add a duplicate subscriber merely to render a chat message. Application schema transforms do not rewrite message data.\\n34: - For Application Event subscriptions, reuse an existing schema module; otherwise create an application-owned module relative to actual sourceRoot (for example `agent-contract/agent-events.ts`) and wire it through `Agent.appEventSchemas`. That directory is optional, not a preinstalled Runtime requirement. A Plugin-local schema wired through the application is also valid.\\n35: - Frontend Tools use standard AG-UI `RunAgentInput.tools`, `TOOL_CALL_*`, `ToolMessage`, and a continuation Run. Never encode a frontend Tool call as `CUSTOM`, a new UserMessage, or a Plugin-specific protocol.\\n36: - The Agent calls a capability adapter, never React or a Plugin. Reuse or define a stable Service seam, let a Provider Plugin declare and provide that Service, then expose only the product-authorized operations in `/agent-contract/agent-tools.ts`.\\n37: - Tool names use `lower_snake_case`; schemas use `z.strictObject(...)` as the single validation and JSON Schema source; handlers resolve Services at execution time and return short serializable results.\\n38: - Do not infer that every Service method should be exposed. Capability ownership and Agent exposure permission are separate decisions.\\n39: - Plugins must never self-register Frontend Tools or import AG-UI Tool types. They only provide capabilities through the existing Service lifecycle.\\n40: - Do not invent Application Events to replace standard Activity/Tool/Run lifecycle, persistent shared state, local Plugin communication, or frontend commands. An explicitly supplied business-job CUSTOM notification can drive a live panel through the application channel; it does not become standard Runtime execution progress or gain history/replay. A single Plugin's private projection may use component state and update by the supplied business ID. Subscribe in `useEffect` and return the unsubscribe function. Use existing shared state/services when persistence or cross-Plugin sharing is actually required.\\n41: - A request for different layout or placement is not an AG-UI Runtime change.\\n42: - During Phase 8, implement AG-UI presentation inside `/plugins/` while keeping Runtime and Framework source read-only.\\n43: \\n44: For interaction semantics, reason from product needs and AG-UI first. Treat external Agent UI projects as references, not runtime dependencies or public contracts.\\n45: \\n46: ## History, attachments, speech and feedback services\\n47: \\n48: These capabilities have built-in defaults and project-owned service wiring.\\n49: Business URLs/auth belong in implementation code, not Agent props. Before connecting a business history, upload, transcription or feedback\\n50: service, read [service configuration](references/service-configuration.md).\\n51: Users supply endpoint/authentication/payload details. Creator edits wiring and uses the confirmed private implementation change tools\\n52: for services/ HTTP integration. Explain operation and shared impact before\\n53: confirmation; public Service contract changes use their separate tools. Report\\n54: applied changes and verification accurately. Preserve explicit custom adapters and Plugin layout.\\n55: \\n56: ## Frontend Tool execution and replay contract\\n57: \\n58: For capability or renderer integration details, read the packaged\\n59: [Frontend Tool lifecycle reference](references/frontend-tool-lifecycle.md).\\n60: \\n61: 1. Product side effects belong only in Tool `execute`: open dialog, navigate,\\n62:    select item, change editor file, modify application state, write storage, copy\\n63:    clipboard, download, call mutation API, submit form, or trigger a host action.\\n64: 2. A Tool renderer is a replayable projection. Assume history load, thread/branch\\n65:    switch, remount, React StrictMode and virtualization can mount it again.\\n66:    `render / mount useEffect != tool invocation`.\\n67: 3. Never implement Agent behavior with\\n68:    `useEffect(() => capability.doSomething(), [])`. Instead use\\n69:    `execute -> capability.doSomething()` and `render -> args/result/status`.\\n70:    UI measurement, ResizeObserver, subscription cleanup, focus and animation\\n71:    effects remain legitimate.\\n72: 4. Results must carry a historical receipt sufficient to render args/result/status\\n73:    without checking today's application state. An opened dialog result can contain\\n74:    `{ opened: true, title: \\\"Settings\\\" }`; history does not require it to stay open.\\n75: 5. History restoration must never execute Frontend Tools. An execute on history\\n76:    load is a Runtime bug; do not patch it with renderer `isHistory` workarounds.\\n77: 6. Keep `Plugin -> Service`, `Application agent-tools -> allowed operation`,\\n78:    `Runtime adapter -> Agent UI native frontend execute`. Plugins never\\n79:    register Tools. Preserve root-only execution scope until subagent attribution\\n80:    has its own contract. Do not add another TOOL_CALL executor or CUSTOM protocol.\\n81: \\n82: ## Official integrations\\n83: \\n84: When Agent UI provides a specialized integration:\\n85: \\n86: 1. Inspect the official integration at the project's pinned upstream revision first.\\n87: 2. Reuse its public semantic contracts where possible.\\n88: 3. Do not bypass AgentUICreator application permission.\\n89: 4. Model reusable adaptation as an Integration, not as a Demo Plugin.\\n90: \\n91: React Hook Form: reuse `@za38.03/agent-ui-plugins/form-tools.formTools` through\\n92: `integration/react-hook-form`. Use ordinary RHF `useForm()` plus an application\\n93: Service and `createReactHookFormFrontendTools({ serviceName, fields, expose })`.\\n94: `expose` is mandatory and has no default. Do not call `useAssistantForm()` or\\n95: `aui.modelContext.register(...)`; combining those with the factory creates duplicate\\n96: registration and bypasses application permission.\\n97: \\n98: Integration installation != automatic Agent permission. Only application\\n99: frontend-tool modules included in the generated allowlist expose operations.\\n100: Integrations can be pluginless and tool-less. Demo consumers own UI, field names,\\n101: Service lifecycle and replayable receipts; the Integration owns reusable contracts.\\n102: \\n103: ## Agent UI Generative UI\\n104: \\n105: `@za38.03/agent-ui-plugins/generative-ui` is the canonical component vocabulary and\\n106: renderer. Prefer official vocabulary before creating custom components. Inspect\\n107: the project's installed version: do not infer capabilities from unreleased\\n108: source or from the presence of related CSS selectors.\\n109: \\n110: - Install optional `integration/generative-ui` for the shared factory, official\\n111:   styled vocabulary, Action Registry and public component export. These files\\n112:   are managed together as one optional Integration, not a default Foundation.\\n113: - `createAgentUIGenerativeUI` exposes `present()` and `promptUser()` capabilities.\\n114:   Capability availability does not grant Agent permission. Do not automatically\\n115:   mount or advertise `present` / `prompt_user`; explicit Tool authorization requires a separate product decision.\\n116: - Use the pinned package types and official Form `$input` collection; do not infer availability from CSS selectors.\\n117: - Keep optional source provenance and official styles under Source Registry;\\n118:   regenerate from the pinned package-owned upstream source. CSS permits only\\n119:   mechanical `.agent-ui-conversation` selector scoping, with no visual redesign.\\n120: \\n121: ## Localization ownership\\n122: \\n123: Vendor-owned code must remain locale-agnostic and unmodified; all product\\n124: localization is owned by AgentUICreator composition, adapters, plugins, and Host\\n125: UI. Reuse existing official domain copy through `useAgentUILocale(namespace)`;\\n126: `Agent.locales` overrides only existing keys, not arbitrary new namespaces. For\\n127: new business copy, reuse or create a project-owned shared messages module with\\n128: domain namespaces and complete `en-US` / `zh-CN` parity. Select messages there\\n129: with the unified `useAgentUILocaleCode()` value and pass messages to presentation\\n130: helpers; do not create per-Plugin i18n hooks, language branches or another locale\\n131: state. See the packaged Plugin API's localization example. Preserve protocol IDs, enums, schema and persisted fields;\\n132: locale is a view concern. Prefer public props/composition, then owned adapters.\\n133: If upstream lacks a seam, record a localization gap instead of modifying vendor.\\n134: Include `pnpm check:i18n` where available and check new copy during upgrades.\\n135: \",\"hash\":\"6b4b6349d8b3e3864a1f1581b7dad4a8f5ee1b85202ba5ff1ba7aeced3fef483\",\"totalLines\":135,\"nextOffset\":null}}","role":"tool"}

data: {"type":"TOOL_CALL_START","toolCallId":"call_713a651bf16b4f5d9c7a7b48","toolCallName":"read_file"}

data: {"type":"TOOL_CALL_ARGS","toolCallId":"call_713a651bf16b4f5d9c7a7b48","delta":"{\"file_path\":\"/skills/ui-plugin-development/SKILL.md\",\"limit\":250,\"offset\":0}"}

data: {"type":"TOOL_CALL_END","toolCallId":"call_713a651bf16b4f5d9c7a7b48"}

data: {"type":"TOOL_CALL_RESULT","toolCallId":"call_713a651bf16b4f5d9c7a7b48","messageId":"4d0d6bc0-f266-470f-bce7-12dbc3823299","content":"{\"ok\":true,\"result\":{\"path\":\"/skills/ui-plugin-development/SKILL.md\",\"content\":\"1: ---\\n2: name: ui-plugin-development\\n3: description: Edit project-owned UI Plugin code directly. Ask only before creating a new Plugin identity or first taking over an npm implementation.\\n4: compatibility: Agent UI Plugin Creator Phase 8 permits writes under project plugins and AppUIModel composition.\\n5: ---\\n6: \\n7: # UI Plugin development\\n8: \\n9: This Skill applies to frontend Plugin source/application authoring. The system\\n10: prompt owns general evidence, authorization and completion rules; this Skill\\n11: adds Plugin-specific contracts, not an additional investigation checklist.\\n12: Use the smallest relevant section/reference. A resolved existing target does not\\n13: require a full capability inventory, a new UI-system decision or a nearest\\n14: consumer example before it can be modified.\\n15: \\n16: ## Ownership and admission\\n17: \\n18: Project-owned application props, children, renderers and scoped styles are ordinary\\n19: editable code. Inspect the exact target and any concrete unknown contract; edit\\n20: within the user's request. Public npm component use does not require implementation\\n21: source, extraction or takeover. Existing source-owned Plugin edits keep the current\\n22: ownership, upstream provenance and BASE; do not repeat successful takeover consent.\\n23: Only actual npm implementation edits require `takeover_plugin_source` and its\\n24: explicit user agreement. Canonical Runtime/Context, vendor and generated registries\\n25: remain protected. A thin wrapper's omitted public seam is not justification for\\n26: requiring takeover.\\n27: \\n28: Ask `prepare_ui_plugin_development` once before creating a NEW Plugin identity,\\n29: including wrapping a component as a new Plugin. After confirmation, implement\\n30: its source, supporting modules, existing parent changes, composition and tests.\\n31: The question result is not a per-file allowlist; changed implementation details\\n32: never require the same development question again. Existing Plugin behavior,\\n33: new interactions and project Service files are ordinary edits. Only actual npm\\n34: implementation changes need the separate source-takeover question.\\n35: For new Plugin manifest/placement and delivery details consult only relevant\\n36: sections of [authoring contracts](references/authoring-contracts.md).\\n37: \\n38: ## Local change evidence\\n39: \\n40: Read the existing files that will change, using the actual supplied sourceRoot and\\n41: owner paths. `manifest.json` declares identity/data/capabilities; `definition.ts`\\n42: joins manifest and component; `index.tsx` owns composition/behavior; imported\\n43: stylesheets own presentation. Read a neighboring consumer only for wiring that\\n44: is actually unknown. An npm implementation need not appear in local search.\\n45: \\n46: `inspect_ui_plugin` returns application ownership, public APIs and parent semantic\\n47: composition where declared. sharedModule selects the installed public module;\\n48: use the root module for presentation controls and /contracts for Plugin types.\\n49: Use returned parameterContracts; follow exact parameterQueries only when a required\\n50: signature is missing. `$element` accesses an array/index element, not React props.\\n51: Query concrete exported types for overloaded Hooks, rather than repeatedly\\n52: querying `$return`. Shared-symbol queries return API evidence, not an edit target.\\n53: An unavailable guessed symbol is not a missing capability. Preserve declared\\n54: composition slots when appending controls; replacement props can replace them.\\n55: \\n56: Public types establish invocation shape, not rendered styling. Package-owned\\n57: presentationContract describes raw versus styled components, asset requirements\\n58: and Portal ownership. For a visual defect follow the current owned entry's CSS\\n59: imports and inspect the relevant cascade. Re-querying types cannot establish\\n60: centering. Public overlay composites may own their own Portal and Backdrop;\\n61: respect that composition and retain the existing AgentUIRoot theme container.\\n62: Use the public Trigger/Close/focus APIs. Local dialog state or scoped popup\\n63: positioning does not introduce a Service, Agent action, Runtime replacement or\\n64: source takeover. Verify requested placement, closing and focus in the actual Host.\\n65: \\n66: ## Editing constraints\\n67: \\n68: Freshly read existing edit targets in the current run; old messages/handoff are\\n69: navigation, not current read evidence. `edit_file_from_read` edits a stable range;\\n70: `edit_file` handles small local changes; `mutate_ui_plugin_source` makes one atomic\\n71: Plugin-local transaction with edit/create entries. Re-read and reconcile affected\\n72: files after stale-content errors. Do not hand-edit `registry.generated.ts`, the\\n73: AppUIModel JSON or composition-revision metadata; use their Host tools.\\n74: \\n75: Preserve the target's UI library, enabled capabilities, scope bridge, semantic\\n76: slots and original approval/interrupt/error renderers. New user/screen-reader copy\\n77: uses the unified domain locale namespace with en-US/zh-CN parity; never patch\\n78: vendor for localization. Read locale targets when changing them, not for every\\n79: style edit. Official keys use `useAgentUILocale(namespace)` and `Agent.locales`;\\n80: new business keys belong in the project's shared namespaced messages module,\\n81: selected with the unified `useAgentUILocaleCode()` value. The official Hook is\\n82: not an arbitrary namespace registry. Do not invent per-Plugin i18n hooks, locale\\n83: state or Service abstractions. Follow the packaged Plugin API example.\\n84: \\n85: Styles must be imported and scoped to owned classes/data attributes; no global\\n86: resets, Host ancestor selectors, Runtime/Portal changes or vendor overrides.\\n87: Own box-sizing, min-width and responsive maximum size. Flex/grid children must\\n88: shrink and long text wrap; overlays need bounded dimensions appropriate to the\\n89: actual container. Static heuristics are not visual acceptance. The specialized\\n90: responsive-repair proposal tool is not currently exposed by any Creator tool\\n91: group. For ordinary authorized responsive CSS changes, freshly read the owned\\n92: stylesheet, use the available file-edit tools and validate the current revision.\\n93: If the user explicitly requests the unavailable specialized proposal tool,\\n94: explain that limitation; do not claim to have invoked it.\\n95: \\n96: ## Validation and outcome\\n97: \\n98: Validate source changes at the current revision; include the build for new visual\\n99: Plugins or dependency/style integration as required. Composition changes use fresh\\n100: AppUIModel evidence and normal Host mutation/verification tools. Creation/catalog\\n101: synchronization is not mounting: newly created capabilities must be composed when\\n102: requested, and the final revision validated. Consume checks already performed by\\n103: the Host rather than duplicating them. Respect returned repair limits.\\n104: `validate_creator_changes` synchronizes changed Plugin manifest/definition files\\n105: into `registry.generated.ts` before checking. It is the exposed synchronization\\n106: entry; do not search for an npm script or edit the generated registry. Refresh\\n107: composition after synchronization to discover the new capability before mounting.\\n108: \\n109: Keep static, Runtime and browser evidence distinct. Current tests/types or empty\\n110: Runtime errors do not prove popup geometry, keyboard behavior or focus. Validate\\n111: the requested interactions using available authorized browser/test evidence; report\\n112: missing infrastructure or stale evidence precisely. Do not label incomplete work\\n113: as already satisfied or reopen unrelated discovery when verification is unavailable.\\n114: \",\"hash\":\"1d8ac128dcb7bc83dba4ce277e52f3edd6adef5696c134a403a5eb3a22fbec64\",\"totalLines\":114,\"nextOffset\":null}}","role":"tool"}

